<Base title="About" description="How the Atlas of Justice works, where the pictures come from and how to add a case.">
  <section class="about">
    <h1>About the Atlas</h1>
    <p>
      The Atlas collects trials and hearings from many countries. Each case has a place on the map, a short
      description, the outcome and a list of sources. Cameras are often barred from courtrooms, so for many
      famous cases the public knew the proceedings through the drawings of court artists. Where cameras were
      admitted, or where the case left its own photographs, the file uses those instead.
    </p>

    <h2>The pictures</h2>
    <p>
      Every case on the map has one main picture. It is a courtroom drawing where court artists were the public's
      eyes, and a photograph from the courtroom or the case materials (a press photograph, a booking photograph, an
      exhibit) where one exists. For the best-documented cases a photograph is the better record, and the
      caption and the note on the frame always say which kind of picture it is. Pictures are not copied to this
      site: each one is loaded directly from the archive that hosts it (Wikimedia Commons, the Library of Congress,
      a university collection) and is shown with its author, a credit line and a link back to the original record.
      Rights differ between archives and between works, and the credit line says what the host archive states. If
      you hold rights to a picture and want it removed, open an issue in the repository.
    </p>

    <h2>The 18+ edition</h2>
    <p>
      The switch in the header turns on the 18+ edition after an age check. It adds more cases, plus extra
      sections marked <strong>18+</strong> inside files that are open to everyone. These describe killings,
      torture and sexual violence in plain language drawn from court records and reliable reporting, and some files
      add photographs from the case materials (mugshots, exhibits, courtroom pictures). There are no crime-scene or
      victim photographs. The switch is a reader's choice stored in the browser, not a security barrier.
    </p>

    <h2>The map</h2>
    <p>
      A pin marks the city or district of the court, not a street address. Base map: OpenStreetMap contributors.
    </p>

    <h2>Adding a case</h2>
    <p>
      Each case is one Markdown file in <code>src/content/cases/</code>: data at the top, the write-up below. A case is
      shown only if it has an <code>image</code> block with the URL of the picture. Add <code>mature: true</code>
      to show a case only in the 18+ edition, and wrap a section in <code>&lt;section class="adult-only"&gt;</code> (blank lines
      around the Markdown inside) to hide it outside it.
    </p>
    <pre><code>---
title: Name of the proceeding
country: Country
city: City
court: Name of the court
region: europe        # europe, north-america, south-america, africa, asia, oceania
category: political   # murder, serial, mob, assassination, terror, spies, political, war,
                      # fraud, celebrity, wrongful, landmark, inquiry
lat: 48.85
lng: 2.35
yearStart: 1899
yearEnd: 1906         # optional
summary: One or two sentences for the card (240 characters at most).
outcome: How it ended.
verdict: guilty       # optional stamp: guilty, acquitted, overturned, mixed, hung,
                      # dismissed, settled, ruling, inquiry
glance:               # optional "at a glance" box
  defendants: Who stood trial
  charges: What they were charged with
  judge: Who presided
timeline:             # optional
  - date: 1 Jan 1900
    event: What happened
related: [other-case-slug]   # optional
image:
  src: https://example.org/picture.jpg
  kind: drawing         # optional: drawing (default), photo, other
  alt: What the picture shows
  artist: Artist or photographer
  license: Credit / rights note
  sourceUrl: https://example.org/record
sources:
  - title: Source title
    url: https://example.org/source
---

The write-up: ## The case, ## In the courtroom, ## Aftermath.</code></pre>
  </section>
</Base>
